1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,504
A running leg.

2
00:00:01,504 --> 00:00:02,511
A green leaf.

3
00:00:02,511 --> 00:00:03,947
A tomato turning red.

4
00:00:04,347 --> 00:00:07,470
A green tomato sat on the
kitchen shelf on Monday.

5
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:09,959
By Thursday, it had turned bright red.

6
00:00:10,409 --> 00:00:12,336
Diya noticed it while making lunch.

7
00:00:12,336 --> 00:00:16,426
She wants to be a doctor, and she
asked: where did the green go, and where

8
00:00:16,426 --> 00:00:17,774
did the red come from?

9
00:00:18,124 --> 00:00:22,380
The answer is a tiny part inside
plant cells, called a plastid.

10
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:26,788
And it has a partner in almost
every cell, called a mitochondrion.

11
00:00:27,288 --> 00:00:31,667
By the end, you will see inside both,
find three kinds of plastid, and

12
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:33,871
know which one makes energy usable.

13
00:00:34,521 --> 00:00:35,984
First, two new words.

14
00:00:36,334 --> 00:00:41,172
An organelle is a tiny part inside a
cell with its own job, just like an

15
00:00:41,172 --> 00:00:42,856
organ inside your body.

16
00:00:43,306 --> 00:00:46,511
Mitochondria and plastids
are two special organelles.

17
00:00:46,511 --> 00:00:49,590
Each one is wrapped in
two membranes, not one.

18
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:54,200
A membrane is a thin skin,
like the skin of a soap bubble.

19
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,175
Two skins, one inside the
other, make a double membrane.

20
00:00:58,825 --> 00:01:02,086
Let's zoom into a cell,
one level at a time.

21
00:01:02,436 --> 00:01:04,372
A cell holds many mitochondria.

22
00:01:04,372 --> 00:01:06,971
They are shaped like
sausages or cylinders.

23
00:01:06,971 --> 00:01:10,492
A busy cell has more of them,
a resting cell has fewer.

24
00:01:10,992 --> 00:01:12,915
Zoom into one mitochondrion.

25
00:01:12,915 --> 00:01:16,261
It has an outer membrane,
and an inner membrane.

26
00:01:16,261 --> 00:01:20,076
Between them, the inside is
split into two watery rooms.

27
00:01:20,576 --> 00:01:23,853
The inner membrane folds
inward, again and again.

28
00:01:23,853 --> 00:01:25,951
These folds are called cristae.

29
00:01:25,951 --> 00:01:29,698
Folding gives much more surface
area, more room to work.

30
00:01:30,198 --> 00:01:32,356
Zoom into the matrix, the inner room.

31
00:01:32,356 --> 00:01:36,127
It holds a single circular DNA
molecule, a few RNA molecules, and small

32
00:01:36,127 --> 00:01:37,931
ribosomes, called 70S ribosomes.

33
00:01:37,931 --> 00:01:40,920
Mitochondria divide by splitting
in two, called fission.

34
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:45,487
Now watch what a mitochondrion
does, one step at a time.

35
00:01:45,837 --> 00:01:46,737
Step one.

36
00:01:46,650 --> 00:01:49,632
Food, like sugar, reaches
the mitochondrion.

37
00:01:50,382 --> 00:01:51,291
Step two.

38
00:01:51,291 --> 00:01:52,943
Oxygen comes in too.

39
00:01:52,943 --> 00:01:57,198
Using oxygen to release energy
is called aerobic respiration.

40
00:01:57,948 --> 00:01:58,848
Step three.

41
00:01:58,835 --> 00:02:02,653
The food is broken down, step
by step, along the cristae.

42
00:02:03,403 --> 00:02:04,303
Step four.

43
00:02:04,081 --> 00:02:06,764
The energy is packed into
small molecules called ATP.

44
00:02:06,764 --> 00:02:08,566
Think of ATP as charged batteries.

45
00:02:09,316 --> 00:02:10,216
Step five.

46
00:02:10,122 --> 00:02:12,886
ATP travels out, and
powers the whole cell.

47
00:02:12,886 --> 00:02:16,799
That is why mitochondria are called
the power houses of the cell.

48
00:02:17,549 --> 00:02:18,609
Now the plastids.

49
00:02:18,609 --> 00:02:21,590
They are found in all plant
cells, and in euglenoids.

50
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:24,517
Plastids are named by
the pigment they carry.

51
00:02:24,517 --> 00:02:26,471
A pigment is a coloured chemical.

52
00:02:26,471 --> 00:02:28,979
Chloroplasts carry
chlorophyll and carotenoids.

53
00:02:28,979 --> 00:02:33,156
They trap light energy for photosynthesis,
the way a plant makes its food.

54
00:02:33,656 --> 00:02:36,966
Inside a chloroplast, the
space is called the stroma.

55
00:02:36,966 --> 00:02:40,566
Flat sacs called thylakoids are
stacked like piles of coins.

56
00:02:40,566 --> 00:02:42,156
Each stack is a granum.

57
00:02:42,156 --> 00:02:45,176
Flat tubes called stroma
lamellae join the stacks.

58
00:02:45,676 --> 00:02:49,999
A green alga called Chlamydomonas
has just one chloroplast per cell.

59
00:02:49,999 --> 00:02:53,148
A leaf mesophyll cell
can have twenty to forty.

60
00:02:53,648 --> 00:02:56,431
Chromoplasts carry carotenoid
pigments, like carotene.

61
00:02:56,431 --> 00:02:58,731
They give yellow, orange and red colours.

62
00:02:58,731 --> 00:02:59,988
That is Diya's tomato.

63
00:02:59,988 --> 00:03:03,798
As it ripens, its chloroplasts turn
into chromoplasts, and the green gives

64
00:03:03,798 --> 00:03:04,698
way to red.

65
00:03:05,098 --> 00:03:06,469
Leucoplasts have no colour.

66
00:03:06,469 --> 00:03:07,385
They store food.

67
00:03:07,385 --> 00:03:09,561
Amyloplasts store
starch, like in a potato.

68
00:03:09,561 --> 00:03:11,163
Elaioplasts store oils and fats.

69
00:03:11,163 --> 00:03:12,526
Aleuroplasts store proteins.

70
00:03:13,176 --> 00:03:14,115
Side by side.

71
00:03:14,115 --> 00:03:15,015
Where.

72
00:03:14,644 --> 00:03:18,648
Mitochondria are found in almost all
eukaryotic cells, plant and animal.

73
00:03:18,648 --> 00:03:21,584
Chloroplasts are found in
plant cells and euglenoids.

74
00:03:22,334 --> 00:03:23,581
The inner membrane.

75
00:03:23,581 --> 00:03:26,038
In a mitochondrion it folds into cristae.

76
00:03:26,038 --> 00:03:29,886
In a chloroplast it wraps the
stroma, which holds the thylakoids.

77
00:03:30,636 --> 00:03:31,536
The job.

78
00:03:31,284 --> 00:03:33,871
Mitochondria release
energy from food, as ATP.

79
00:03:33,871 --> 00:03:36,289
Chloroplasts trap light
energy to make food.

80
00:03:37,039 --> 00:03:37,939
Your turn.

81
00:03:37,879 --> 00:03:42,201
A potato stores lots of starch,
deep under the soil, in the dark.

82
00:03:42,201 --> 00:03:44,178
Which plastid fills its cells?

83
00:03:44,178 --> 00:03:45,413
Pause and decide.

84
00:03:48,613 --> 00:03:49,513
Amyloplasts!

85
00:03:49,209 --> 00:03:51,717
They are leucoplasts, colourless
plastids that store starch.

86
00:03:52,367 --> 00:03:53,299
Remember this.

87
00:03:53,299 --> 00:03:54,893
Mitochondria release energy.

88
00:03:54,893 --> 00:03:56,182
Chloroplasts trap it.

89
00:03:56,782 --> 00:03:58,502
Now, a worked example.

90
00:03:58,502 --> 00:03:59,402
Six parts.

91
00:03:59,387 --> 00:04:01,300
Mitochondrion or chloroplast?

92
00:04:01,650 --> 00:04:04,473
Cristae, the folds of the inner membrane.

93
00:04:04,473 --> 00:04:05,484
Mitochondrion.

94
00:04:05,934 --> 00:04:07,533
Thylakoids, the flat sacs.

95
00:04:07,533 --> 00:04:08,433
Chloroplast.

96
00:04:08,753 --> 00:04:10,937
The matrix with its circular DNA.

97
00:04:10,937 --> 00:04:11,914
Mitochondrion.

98
00:04:12,364 --> 00:04:14,442
Grana, the stacks of coins.

99
00:04:14,442 --> 00:04:15,388
Chloroplast.

100
00:04:15,838 --> 00:04:17,892
Chlorophyll, the green pigment.

101
00:04:17,892 --> 00:04:18,792
Chloroplast.

102
00:04:19,215 --> 00:04:21,652
And ATP made in aerobic respiration.

103
00:04:21,652 --> 00:04:22,673
Mitochondrion.

104
00:04:23,324 --> 00:04:24,785
Now, the exam trap.

105
00:04:25,135 --> 00:04:27,932
Many students think the ribosomes
inside mitochondria and chloroplasts

106
00:04:27,932 --> 00:04:30,229
are 80S, like the ones
in the rest of the cell.

107
00:04:30,629 --> 00:04:31,773
But they are smaller.

108
00:04:31,773 --> 00:04:33,721
The ribosomes in the cytoplasm are 80S.

109
00:04:33,721 --> 00:04:36,188
Inside mitochondria and
chloroplasts, they are 70S.

110
00:04:36,838 --> 00:04:38,254
Where is this asked?

111
00:04:38,254 --> 00:04:42,798
In board papers, as a labelled diagram
of a mitochondrion or a chloroplast,

112
00:04:42,798 --> 00:04:45,868
and in NEET, as statement
questions on plastids.

113
00:04:46,468 --> 00:04:47,614
Exam level now.

114
00:04:47,614 --> 00:04:49,403
Which statement is correct?

115
00:04:49,403 --> 00:04:50,598
Pause and solve.

116
00:04:55,998 --> 00:04:57,334
The answer is C.

117
00:04:57,834 --> 00:05:01,296
Cristae fold from the inner
membrane, not the outer one.

118
00:05:01,296 --> 00:05:04,130
Elaioplasts store oils
and fats, not proteins.

119
00:05:04,630 --> 00:05:07,584
And leucoplasts are colourless,
so they cannot trap light.

120
00:05:07,584 --> 00:05:08,667
Only C is correct.

121
00:05:09,267 --> 00:05:10,807
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:10,807 --> 00:05:12,390
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:15,590 --> 00:05:17,776
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:18,176 --> 00:05:19,076
One.

125
00:05:18,636 --> 00:05:21,276
What are cristae, and why are they folded?

126
00:05:22,176 --> 00:05:23,076
Two.

127
00:05:22,604 --> 00:05:24,457
Name the three kinds of plastid.

128
00:05:25,357 --> 00:05:26,257
Three.

129
00:05:25,882 --> 00:05:27,637
Which leucoplast stores starch?

130
00:05:28,737 --> 00:05:33,274
Next time: how can tiny hairs on a
cell sweep dust out of your windpipe?

131
00:05:33,274 --> 00:05:37,671
We find out in ribosomes, cytoskeleton,
cilia, flagella and centrosome.
